"""Plain-language explanations shared by controls and the dashboard guide."""
CONTROL_HELP={
 'offense_share':'How much a college team’s own offense matters compared with the opponent’s defense. At 65%, its offensive numbers get 65% of the matchup calculation and the opponent’s defensive allowances get 35%. Moving right gives strong offenses more influence; moving left gives opposing defenses more influence.',
 'epa_weight':'Optional NFL scoring blend based on expected points added per play. Zero leaves EPA to the trained model’s features. Moving toward 1 makes a direct EPA forecast replace more of the yards/success forecast; this can count the same efficiency twice, so validate changes.',
 'epa_reference':'The NFL EPA-per-play level treated as average in the optional direct forecast. Raising it lowers both projected scores when the EPA blend is enabled; it has no score effect at a zero blend.',
 'rest_day_weight':'Extra NFL home-margin points per day of rest advantage. At 0.1, home resting three more days adds 0.3 margin points before the cap. Zero leaves rest as a training feature only; negative values reverse the assumption.',
 'rest_cap_points':'The maximum direct NFL rest advantage or penalty in margin points. Raising this permits larger effects; it has no score effect while the rest-day weight is zero.',
 'ml_weight':'CFB only: how much of the trained gradient-boosting correction to add. 0 disables it; 1 uses the validated correction in full. Projected margin and total can both change. Import history and train first. Changing core scoring settings disables the fit until you restore them or retrain.',
 'by':'How much average distance gained on each play affects the score. A higher value makes efficient offenses and weak defenses matter more.',
 'bs':'How much consistently successful plays affect the score. Higher values give more credit to teams that keep moving toward another first down.',
 'shrink':'How strongly to use the original play statistics. Lower values pull that part of the forecast toward an average team; higher values allow larger strength gaps. FEI and result ratings have separate weights.',
 'turnover_retention':'How much past takeaways and giveaways carry forward. 0 ignores their past advantage; 1 keeps the full adjustment. It does not predict the exact number of future turnovers.',
 'turnover_point_value':'The assumed point value of a turnover. Higher values make retained takeaway and giveaway differences affect the score more.',
 'q':'The starting score for an average team at the reference pace, before other adjustments. Raising it increases both teams’ forecasts and usually the total.',
 'bx':'How much big-play ability affects scoring. Higher positive values give more credit to explosive offenses. The NFL and CFB feeds use different big-play measures.',
 'h':'The home team’s assumed advantage in points of winning margin. A value of 2 gives the home team +1 score point and the visitor −1. Neutral sites get no home bonus.',
 'sigma_spread':'How uncertain the winning-margin forecast is, in points. Larger values make spread probabilities less confident; projected scores do not change.',
 'sigma_total':'How uncertain the combined-score forecast is, in points. Larger values make over/under probabilities less confident; projected scores do not change.',
 'n':'The number of offensive plays expected from an average team. A game with more plays than this benchmark raises the pace multiplier and usually scoring.',
 'y':'The average yards per play used as a benchmark. Teams above this level receive an efficiency boost; teams below it receive a reduction.',
 's':'The average successful-play percentage used as a benchmark. Higher team percentages help scoring. 44 means 44%, not 0.44.',
 'x':'The benchmark for the feed’s big-play measure. The NFL uses the percentage of pass plays gaining 20+ yards; CFB uses points added on successful plays.',
 'fei_weight':'CFB only: how much to rely on an efficiency rating that accounts for opponent strength. 0 uses the original play-stat forecast; 1 uses the FEI scoring forecast. Both teams need dated ratings.',
 'fei_drives':'The reference number of possessions each team gets to score. More possessions increase the scoring effect of FEI offense and defense ratings.',
 'starter_weight':'Points assigned to each extra returning starter, before the early-season discount. A starter is a player in the team’s usual starting lineup.',
 'starters_reference':'The returning-starter count considered ordinary for offense or defense. Counts above this receive more continuity credit; counts below it receive less.',
 'portal_weight':'How much incoming-minus-outgoing transfer talent affects the winning margin. Bigger values increase this effect. Missing net ratings are skipped.',
 'portal_scale':'The amount of net transfer-rating difference that represents one unit of influence. Larger values reduce the portal effect; provider scales must be consistent.',
 'roster_half_life':'After this many games, preseason starter and transfer adjustments have half their initial influence. Higher values keep those adjustments relevant longer.',
 'power_weight':'How much to blend prior game results, adjusted for opponents, into the efficiency-based winning margin. 0 ignores this layer; 1 uses its full strength gap. It does not force favorite or underdog picks.',
 'power_ridge':'For the regularized result-rating method, how much to pull ratings toward an average team. Higher values reduce extreme ratings. This setting does not shrink standard Massey least-squares ratings.',
 'power_min_games':'Each team needs at least this many prior completed games before the result-rating layer is used. More games provide more evidence but delay early-season use.',
 'ats_weight':'How strongly past spread-covering rates at the current venue affect the margin. At 2, a 10 percentage-point difference in the smoothed rates adds 0.2 points before the cap.',
 'ats_prior_games':'How much to pull historical ATS percentages toward 50%. A value of 40 adds the equivalent of 20 covers and 20 non-covers, preventing small samples from dominating.',
 'ats_cap_points':'The most that the ATS-history layer can change the winning margin, in either direction. It does not change the combined score.',
 'h2h_margin_weight':'How much old meetings between these two teams affect the winning margin. History is discounted for age and small samples before applying this weight.',
 'h2h_total_weight':'How much old meetings affect the combined score. Larger values use their historical scoring pattern more strongly, after discounts.',
 'h2h_half_life_years':'How quickly old meetings lose influence. A game this many years old gets half the weight of a recent meeting.',
 'h2h_prior_games':'How cautious to be with a small number of past meetings. Larger values pull the history adjustment closer to zero.',
 'h2h_difference_cap':'The largest historical-versus-current forecast difference allowed into the history formula, before its small-sample and weight discounts.',
 'lookahead_margin_points':'Change in a team’s winning margin if it has a major opponent next. A negative number tests distraction; a positive number tests a benefit. Zero gives no effect.',
 'big_win_margin_points':'Change in a team’s winning margin after a major win. Negative tests a letdown; positive tests a confidence boost. Zero gives no effect.',
 'road_loss_home_margin_points':'Extra margin points for a home team whose previous game was a road loss. This is separate from ordinary home-field advantage.',
 'lookahead_total_points':'Change in the combined score for each team with a look-ahead flag. Positive raises scoring; negative lowers it. Both flags can contribute.',
 'big_win_total_points':'Change in the combined score for each team coming off a major win. This tests a scoring scenario, not an established effect.',
 'context_window_days':'The maximum gap in days for a previous or next game to count as schedule context. A bye longer than this removes its effect.',
 'major_opponent_points':'How strong an opponent must be, relative to an average team, to count as a major game. A higher threshold flags fewer opponents.',
 'context_cap_points':'The maximum combined schedule-context change to the margin or total. It limits how much several situational assumptions can add up.',
 'margin_scale':'A multiplier on the model’s team-strength gap. Above 1 makes expected wins/losses bigger; below 1 makes games closer. Use later-game validation to choose it.',
 'margin_intercept':'A fixed shift to the visitor’s winning margin. Positive favors the visitor; negative favors the home team. It should come from a validated calibration.',
 'total_scale':'A multiplier on the model’s scoring total, excluding separate game modifiers. Above 1 raises scoring; below 1 lowers it.',
 'total_intercept':'A fixed number of points added to the combined score. Positive raises the total; negative lowers it.',
 'minimum_ev':'Show only quoted markets whose estimated average net return exceeds this percentage under the model assumptions. Raising it filters the display; it does not improve the forecasts.',
}

METRIC_HELP={
 'epa':'NFL offense: expected points added per play. Higher means plays improved scoring chances more. This accounts for field position and down/distance.',
 'depa':'NFL defense: opponent EPA per play. Lower means the defense allowed less improvement in opponent scoring chances.',
 'pass_epa':'NFL offense: EPA per dropback, including sacks and quarterback scrambles when dropback data is available.',
 'dpass_epa':'NFL defense: opponent EPA per dropback. Lower is better.',
 'rush_epa':'NFL offense: EPA per designed rushing play. Higher is better.',
 'drush_epa':'NFL defense: opponent EPA per designed rushing play. Lower is better.',
 'cpoe':'NFL completion percentage over expected, in percentage points. +3 means completing about three more passes per 100 attempts than expected from play difficulty.',
 'sack_rate':'NFL offense: sacks taken per 100 dropbacks. Lower means fewer lost passing opportunities.',
 'dsack_rate':'NFL defense: sacks forced per 100 opponent dropbacks. Higher means more disruption; this is not a full pressure rate.',
 'y':'Offense: average yards gained on each play.', 'dy':'Defense: average yards allowed on each opponent play. Lower is generally better.',
 's':'Offense: percentage of successful plays (0–100). CFB uses down-and-distance success; NFL uses positive expected-points change.',
 'ds':'Defense: opponent successful-play percentage (0–100). Lower is generally better.',
 'x':'Offensive big-play measure: NFL 20+ yard pass percentage; CFB points added on successful plays.',
 'dx':'Opponent big-play measure allowed by the defense, using the same league definition as x.',
 'p':'Average offensive plays per game. More plays usually mean more scoring opportunities.',
 't':'Net takeaways minus giveaways per game. Positive means the team took the ball away more often than it lost it.',
}

SCENARIO_HELP={
 'line_away':'Visitor’s point handicap. +10 means the visitor can lose by fewer than 10 and still cover; −10 means it needs to win by more than 10.',
 'total':'The market’s combined score threshold. Over needs more points; under needs fewer. An equal integer total is a push.',
 'injury_away':'Points added to the visitor’s score for an injury scenario. Use a negative number for a missing player who reduces expected scoring.',
 'injury_home':'Points added to the home team’s score for an injury scenario. Negative lowers its expected scoring.',
 'rest_margin_home':'Extra rest/travel advantage for the home team, in margin points. Positive favors home; negative favors away. The total stays the same.',
 'weather_total':'Change to combined scoring for weather. For example, −4 takes 2 points off each team’s score.',
}
